  "hero.title": "How V8 Runs JavaScript",
  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Software principles · A hands-on long essay",
  "hero.sub": "Type <code>node bench.js</code> in a terminal, and within milliseconds hot loops run at native machine speed.<br>Dynamic objects carry no static field offsets—so how does V8 dare read memory at fixed offsets, and when does it bail back out?",
  "hero.lead": "JavaScript ships with zero static type annotations. The same `p.x + p.y` in a helper function might receive small integer points today, heap floats tomorrow, sparse objects missing a key, or even strings. V8 cannot afford hundreds of milliseconds of upfront ahead-of-time compilation on startup, nor can it walk a hash dictionary on every property access. This essay follows one question from end to end: <strong>what happens between handing a `.js` file to V8 and hot loops executing native machine code while memory gets reclaimed cleanly?</strong> We walk through the exact lifecycle: how code climbs the four-tier pipeline, how values pack into tagged words, how dynamic objects grow fixed hidden classes and inline caches, how Ignition feeds runtime types into its FeedbackVector, why a single broken guard reconstructs an interpreter stack frame, why arrays degrade one way down a lattice, how generational GC keeps most pauses under a millisecond, and where V8 hands control back to the host event loop.",
  "hero.note": "Thresholds, byte sizes, bytecode disassembly, and debug prints here were all verified directly against <strong>V8 14.6.202.34-node.40</strong> bundled in <strong>Node.js v26.12.1</strong> on macOS arm64. Where Chrome's browser builds differ—specifically enabling <strong>Pointer Compression</strong> and the <strong>V8 Heap Sandbox</strong>—the prose marks both, because compile-time flags change Smi bit widths and object byte sizes directly. The page embeds no external engine: nine interactive models in `models.js` replay the rules deterministically, and `tools/crosscheck.cjs` executes `/opt/homebrew/bin/node` with `--print-bytecode`, `--trace-opt`, `--trace-deopt`, and `--allow-natives-syntax` to check every number. For the underlying CPU hardware, see <a href=\"../../systems/how-a-cpu-runs-a-program/\">How a CPU Runs a Program</a>, <a href=\"../../systems/how-cpu-caches-work/\">How a CPU's Three Levels of Cache Work</a>, <a href=\"../../systems/how-a-cpu-guesses-ahead/\">How a CPU Guesses Ahead</a>, and <a href=\"../../systems/how-a-cpu-does-math/\">How a CPU Does Math: Real Numbers, and Many at Once</a>.",
  "ch1.title": "The 4-tier pipeline: startup latency vs peak throughput",
  "toc.ch1": "The 4-tier pipeline & lazy parsing",
  "ch1.law": "V8 never begins with its heaviest compiler. When a script first loads, most functions run once or never; V8 starts executing immediately and only sends a function up the compiler ladder after it proves it is hot.",
  "ch1.p1": "Consider this ten-line script launched with `node bench.js`. A hot loop pushes a hundred thousand point reads through `addXY(p)`, leaving an unused cold helper above it and a resolved `Promise` at the end:",
  "ch1.code.aria": "A minimal JavaScript program that exercises V8's pipeline",
  "ch1.code": "// node bench.js\nfunction coldHelper(v) { return v * 2 + 1; }\nfunction addXY(p) { return p.x + p.y; }\n\nconst pts = Array.from({ length: 1000 }, (_, i) => ({ x: i, y: i + 1 }));\nlet total = 0;\nfor (let r = 0; r < 200; r++) {\n  for (let i = 0; i < pts.length; i++) total += addXY(pts[i]);\n}\nPromise.resolve(total).then(v => console.log(v));",
  "ch1.p2": "Even parsing is deferred. Because `coldHelper` is never called at startup, V8's <strong>PreParser</strong> only scans its braces for syntax validity and skips building an AST or bytecode—this is <strong>lazy parsing</strong>. Only functions that actually run (`addXY`) undergo full parsing into <strong>Ignition</strong> bytecode. As calls accumulate in V8 14.6.202.34-node.40, execution climbs four tiers: around 8 calls V8 allocates a FeedbackVector and <strong>Sparkplug</strong> compiles bytecode straight into unoptimized baseline machine code; at 100 loop iterations (`--invocation-count-for-maglev-osr`) or 400 calls (`--invocation-count-for-maglev`), <strong>Maglev</strong> takes over for fast mid-tier optimization; at 500 OSR iterations or 3,000 calls, <strong>TurboFan / Turboshaft</strong> spends full compiler effort on peak throughput.",
  "dg.pipeline.cap": "Figure 1. V8's four-tier execution pipeline and deoptimization return path. Code climbs left-to-right in heat; the dashed red path bails directly back to Ignition bytecode if an assumption fails.",
  "dg.pipeline.aria": "Diagram: five stages from Parser and PreParser, Ignition bytecode, Sparkplug, Maglev, to TurboFan/Turboshaft with a red deoptimization return path back to Ignition",
