  "ch2.title": "Pointer tagging & compression: why some numbers live on the heap",
  "toc.ch2": "Pointer tagging & compression",
  "ch2.law": "Every JavaScript value in a stack slot or object field must fit into one uniform \"tagged word.\" V8 inspects its lowest bit: `0` means an immediate signed integer (`Smi`) packed straight inside the word, while `1` marks a pointer to a heap-allocated object.",
  "ch2.p1": "Because registers and object slots cannot tell statically whether a field holds an integer or a string next millisecond, V8 tags every value. Storing raw 64-bit pointers everywhere wastes half the L1/L2 cache lines on identical upper bits (see <a href=\"../../systems/how-cpu-caches-work/#ch2\">cache line packing</a>). Chrome therefore builds V8 with <strong>Pointer Compression</strong>: the entire heap sits inside a contiguous 4 GiB virtual address <strong>cage</strong> whose base stays pinned in a CPU register, shrinking every heap reference to a 32-bit offset. When the low bit is `0`, the upper 31 bits hold an immediate <strong>Smi</strong> (`-1,073,741,824` to `1,073,741,823`); when it is `1`, the offset locates a `HeapObject` in the cage. Any fractional value like `0.5` or an integer past the Smi limit forces V8 to allocate a <strong>HeapNumber</strong> box holding a 64-bit IEEE-754 double (see <a href=\"../../systems/how-a-cpu-does-math/#ch1\">IEEE-754 floats</a>).",
  "ch2.p2": "Compile flags matter here. On our host Node.js v26.12.1 (V8 14.6.202.34-node.40), pointer compression is turned off (`v8_enable_pointer_compression = 0`), leaving full 64-bit tagged words: Smis fill the upper 32 bits (up to `2,147,483,647`), a boxed `HeapNumber` occupies 16 bytes (8-byte `Map` word + 8-byte float64), and a two-field `{x, y}` object takes 40 bytes. Under Chrome's 32-bit pointer compression, that same `{x, y}` object halves to 20 bytes, a `HeapNumber` takes 12 bytes, and the heap caps out at ~4.09 GiB inside its cage.",
  "dg.tagging.cap": "Figure 2. Tagged word layouts across two V8 build modes: 32-bit compressed offsets in a 4 GiB cage (top, Chrome) vs 64-bit full tagged words verified on host Node v26.12.1 (bottom), showing HeapNumber boxing.",
  "dg.tagging.aria": "Diagram: 32-bit pointer-compressed Smi/HeapObject bit fields and 64-bit full Smi/HeapNumber pointer layout",
