  "ch2.say.heap": "Value {val} cannot fit as a Smi (tag bit becomes <em>1</em>): V8 must follow the pointer to a {heap}-byte <code>HeapNumber</code> box on the heap, creating GC work for every escaping float result.",
  "ch2.limit": "The model computes standalone value representations and two-field object headers without scalar replacement—where Maglev or TurboFan eliminates non-escaping `HeapNumber` allocations completely inside registers (covered in chapter 6).",
  "ch3.title": "Hidden classes (Maps) & property layout: turning dynamic names into offsets",
  "toc.ch3": "Hidden classes, slack tracking & dicts",
  "ch3.law": "JavaScript objects have no compile-time struct declarations, yet V8 builds shared structural blueprints called `Maps` (hidden classes) on the fly. Objects initialized with the same keys in the same order share one `Map`, so `p.x` compiles down to a base pointer plus a fixed byte offset.",
  "ch3.p1": "Every added property advances the object along a `Map` transition tree: empty `{}` (`M0`) transitions on `+x` to `M1`, then on `+y` to `M2`. Any second object initialized with `x` then `y` shares `M2` (`%HaveSameMap(a1, a2)` is `true`), laying `x` and `y` consecutively in fast <strong>in-object slots</strong>. Swap the order to `{y: 2, x: 1}` and the tree forks immediately from `M0` into `M3 -> M4`: even though both objects hold the exact same key names, `%HaveSameMap` turns `false` and their memory offsets swap.",
  "ch3.p2": "Constructors and `delete` reveal two deeper rules. On `new Point(x, y)`, V8 initially grants extra empty slots (<strong>in-object slack tracking</strong>): for the first 7 constructions on host 64-bit Node, `Point` allocates 10 in-object slots (104 bytes) while `construction counter` counts down from 6 to 0; on call 7, V8 trims the `Map` down to the exact 2 fields used (40 bytes). Conversely, calling `delete obj.b` kicks the object off its fast `Map` into `DictionaryProperties` (shrinking the shell to 24 bytes and moving every key into an out-of-line `NameDictionary` hash table).",
  "dg.map-tree.cap": "Figure 3. The hidden class (Map) transition tree and dictionary fallback. Same initialization order shares M2 on the left; reversed order forks M4 on the right; delete detaches into a NameDictionary hash table.",
  "dg.map-tree.aria": "Diagram: Map transition tree branching from M0 to M1/M2 on the left, M3/M4 on the right, and delete falling to dictionary properties at the bottom",
