  "ch4.limit": "Per-load latency numbers are calibrated to host V8 14.6 microbenchmarks on `getX(o)`; in real applications, megamorphic sites also cause branch-misprediction stalls (see <a href=\"../../systems/how-a-cpu-guesses-ahead/#ch1\">How a CPU Guesses Ahead</a>) and prevent Maglev/TurboFan from hoisting field loads out of loops.",
  "ch5.title": "Bytecode & register VM: how Ignition feeds the FeedbackVector",
  "toc.ch5": "Ignition bytecode & FeedbackVector",
  "ch5.law": "Ignition is a register-based bytecode interpreter anchored around one implicit <strong>accumulator register (`acc`)</strong>. Routing values implicitly through `acc` keeps bytecode compact while simultaneously writing observed types into a parallel `FeedbackVector`.",
  "ch5.p1": "Disassembling `addXY(p)` on V8 14.6.202.34-node.40 yields just <strong>13 bytes</strong> across five instructions (even the `sumLoop` loop is only 33 bytes). Notice two details directly visible in the dump: `Parameter count 2` counts both implicit `this` and parameter `p` (`a0`), and almost every hot instruction carries a `FBV[k]` slot index. `GetNamedProperty` loads `p.x` into `acc` while caching the `Map` in `FBV[1]`; `Add r0, FBV[0]` adds while stamping `SignedSmall`, `Number`, or `Any` into `FBV[0]`. Optimizing compilers later read <strong>BytecodeArray + FeedbackVector</strong>, never the raw source text.",
  "dg.ignition.cap": "Figure 5. Ignition's execution loop: compact bytecode writes results through the implicit accumulator (acc) and frame registers while recording live types into FeedbackVector slots.",
