  "ch7.title": "Arrays & the one-way ElementsKind lattice",
  "toc.ch7": "Arrays & the ElementsKind lattice",
  "ch7.law": "Every JavaScript array stores an `ElementsKind` tag inside its `Map` describing how its elements sit in memory. Transitions across this lattice are strictly one-way: once an array takes a hole or a non-number, it can never upgrade back to packed integers.",
  "ch7.p1": "Creating `[1, 2, 3]` starts at the top of the lattice: <strong>`PACKED_SMI_ELEMENTS`</strong>, meaning contiguous inline Smis with no gaps. Calling `.push(1.5)` promotes the whole backing store to <strong>`PACKED_DOUBLE_ELEMENTS`</strong>, storing raw unboxed 64-bit IEEE-754 floats (meaning reading an element out to polymorphic code now boxes it into a `HeapNumber`). Assigning `arr[5] = 2.5` inserts the sentinel `the_hole` at indices 3 and 4, dropping the array permanently into <strong>`HOLEY_DOUBLE_ELEMENTS`</strong>; adding `.push('hi')` degrades it further to <strong>`HOLEY_ELEMENTS`</strong> (boxed pointers plus prototype-chain lookups whenever a loop hits `the_hole`). Even after setting `arr.length = 3` and overwriting `[1, 2, 3]`, `%HasSmiElements` and `%HasHoleyElements` on host V8 14.6 prove <strong>it stays `HOLEY_ELEMENTS` forever</strong>. On our 1,000-element sum benchmark, `PACKED_SMI` takes 0.25 ns/element, `PACKED_DOUBLE` 0.55 ns, `HOLEY_DOUBLE` 1.59 ns, and `DICTIONARY_ELEMENTS` leaps to 9.88 ns.",
  "dg.elements.cap": "Figure 7. V8's strictly one-way ElementsKind lattice. Every transition flows right (more general element types) or down (introducing holes or dictionary backing) and never reverses.",
  "dg.elements.aria": "Diagram: 2x3 lattice of PACKED_SMI, PACKED_DOUBLE, PACKED above HOLEY_SMI, HOLEY_DOUBLE, HOLEY and DICTIONARY_ELEMENTS below",
  "dg.elements.ps": "Packed Smi",
