  "ch7.say.smi": "Array is in its fastest form, <em>PACKED_SMI_ELEMENTS</em>: contiguous integers, zero unboxing, and zero hole/prototype checks.",
  "ch7.say.double": "Mixing a float moved to <em>PACKED_DOUBLE_ELEMENTS</em>: raw 64-bit floats pack tightly, though reading an item into generic code boxes a HeapNumber.",
  "ch7.say.holey": "A gap injected <em>the_hole</em>! Now in <em>{kind}</em>, loops must guard every read against holes that could inherit from <code>Array.prototype</code>.",
  "ch7.say.tagged": "Mixing a string generalized the backing array to <em>{kind}</em> tagged slots, disabling unboxed numeric array loops.",
  "ch7.say.dict": "Sparse index assignment spilled into <em>DICTIONARY_ELEMENTS</em>: V8 abandoned contiguous arrays for a hash table, multiplying loop cost.",
  "ch7.say.stuck": "Truncating back to length 3 with integers <em>still leaves the array stuck in {kind}</em>—V8's lattice never transitions upward.",
  "ch7.limit": "The per-element latency column mirrors host V8 14.6 benchmarks on our machine; tight `0 .. length-1` loops can sometimes eliminate bounds checks in Maglev, yet cannot erase `the_hole` or prototype checks once holey.",
  "ch8.title": "Orinoco generational GC: why pauses stay sub-millisecond or spike",
  "toc.ch8": "Orinoco generational & concurrent GC",
  "ch8.law": "Most JavaScript objects die microseconds after allocation. V8's <strong>Orinoco</strong> collector splits the heap into a small Young Generation cleaned in fractions of a millisecond by copying or MinorMS, and a large Old Generation guarded by concurrent tri-color marking and write barriers.",
  "ch8.p1": "Fresh objects land in <strong>New Space</strong> (initially 512 KiB in a fresh Node process, while `old_space` starts around 2.98 MiB), where bump-pointer allocation just increments an address pointer. When New Space fills, <strong>Scavenger</strong> runs Cheney's semi-space copy: only live objects move from `From-space` to `To-space`, pausing proportionally to survivors alone. Under `--trace-gc` on our host binary, Scavenge pauses measured <strong>0.03–0.21 ms</strong>. Objects surviving two scavenges <strong>promote</strong> into <strong>Old Space</strong>.",
  "ch8.p2": "Copying half of a gigabyte-scale Old Space is prohibitive, so Orinoco manages it via <strong>Mark-Sweep-Compact</strong>: background threads run <strong>tri-color marking</strong> (White = unvisited, Grey = queued, Black = scanned) concurrently alongside JavaScript. If JS mutates a Black object to point to a White object mid-mark, a short <strong>write barrier</strong> before pointer writes marks the White target Grey or updates the `RememberedSet` (Old → New pointers). If rapid promotions outpace concurrent marking, V8 stops the main thread for a Major Mark-Sweep-Compact—spiking pause times into tens of milliseconds.",
  "dg.gc.cap": "Figure 8. Orinoco's generational heap. Young Gen copies rare survivors from From-space to To-space (or marks in-place with MinorMS); survivors promote to OldSpace while write barriers track Old-to-New edges.",
  "dg.gc.aria": "Diagram: YoungGen semi-spaces From and To with promotion arrow down to OldSpace tri-color marking and write barrier back-edge",
