  "ch9.title": "The event loop & microtask seam: when V8 yields back to the host",
  "toc.ch9": "Event loop & microtask seam",
  "ch9.law": "V8 itself knows nothing about `setTimeout`, network sockets, or screen refreshes. V8 owns only the JavaScript call stack and its <strong>MicrotaskQueue</strong>—and the host scheduler (`libuv` in Node or Blink in Chromium) cannot start the next macrotask until V8 drains every microtask.",
  "ch9.p1": "Every turn begins when the host hands one callback to V8. Synchronous code executes until the call stack empties. Before returning control, V8 executes a <strong>MicrotaskCheckpoint</strong>: in Node.js, `process.nextTick` drains first, followed by all queued `Promise.then` and `queueMicrotask` jobs—including any new microtasks queued <em>while</em> draining. On host Node v26.12.1, our opening snippet prints strictly: `main.js (sync) -> nextTick -> Promise.then -> queueMicrotask -> setImmediate -> setTimeout(0)`. That yields a subtle trap: if a `Promise.then` recursively schedules another `Promise.then`, the call stack keeps clearing cleanly, yet V8 never exits `MicrotaskCheckpoint`, starving `setTimeout`, I/O, and UI rendering forever.",
  "dg.eventloop.cap": "Figure 9. The seam between V8 and its host (libuv / browser). The host hands one callback at a time into the call stack, but V8 refuses to return until its MicrotaskCheckpoint empties.",
  "dg.eventloop.aria": "Diagram: V8 Call Stack and MicrotaskCheckpoint on the left communicating across the seam with libuv Timers, Poll, and Check phases on the right",
